      content: 'Key areas to assess for hemispatial neglect:\n- **Awareness of deficits (anosognosia)**: Does the patient acknowledge any left-sided weakness or visual difficulty? Anosognosia is common with right hemisphere strokes and is a major barrier to rehabilitation.\n- **Safety incidents**: Falls, bumping into objects, burns on the left hand from not recognizing hot surfaces, leaving stove burners on.\n- **ADL function**: Does she eat from only the right side of the plate? Can she dress the left side of her body? Does she groom only the right side of her face?\n- **Reading and writing**: Does she miss the left side of text (reading starts mid-line)? This is called \'neglect dyslexia.\'\n- **Pre-stroke function**: Was she independent in all IADLs? Driving history? Employment status?\n- **Mood**: Right hemisphere strokes are associated with emotional dysregulation, flat affect, and aprosodia (inability to convey/interpret emotional tone).\n- **Visual symptoms**: Does she report any vision loss? Patients with pure neglect often deny any visual difficulty, whereas patients with hemianopia may report \'not seeing well on one side.\'\n- **Medications**: Review for any sedating agents that could worsen cognitive function.',
