      content: '**Vitals**: BP 132/76 mmHg, HR 74 bpm (regular), RR 14, SpO2 98% on room air.\n\n**Neurologic Exam**:\n- **Mental Status**: Alert but demonstrates flat affect. Oriented to person, place, and time. Speech fluent but with reduced prosody (monotone). When asked \'How are you doing since the stroke?\' she responds \'Fine, I\'m ready to get back to normal.\'\n- **Visual Fields (Confrontation testing)**: Reduced detection of finger wiggle in the left visual field bilaterally. When tested with simultaneous bilateral stimulation (double simultaneous stimulation/extinction testing), she consistently extinguishes the left-sided stimulus — reports only the right.\n- **Neglect-specific testing**:\n  - **Line bisection test**: Bisects horizontal lines significantly to the RIGHT of true center (average deviation 3.2 cm to the right across 5 lines).\n  - **Star cancellation test**: Crosses out 27/27 stars on the right half of the page but only 8/27 on the left half. Entire left upper quadrant missed.\n  - **Clock drawing**: Numbers 1-8 are crammed into the right half of the clock face. Left half is empty.\n  - **Scene copying**: When asked to copy a simple house drawing, she omits the left wall, left window, and left side of the roof.\n- **Motor**: Left deltoid 4/5, biceps 4/5, wrist extensors 4/5, grip 4/5, hip flexors 4+/5, knee extensors 4+/5, ankle dorsiflexors 4/5. Right side 5/5 throughout.\n- **Sensation**: Reports intact sensation bilaterally when tested individually. However, with double simultaneous stimulation, consistently extinguishes left-sided touch.\n- **Gait**: Ambulates with single-point cane in right hand. Tends to veer leftward (bumps into left wall). Does not scan to the left when turning corners. Near-fall in clinic when she did not see a chair on her left side.',
