    const Section(
      title: '+ Distinguish hemispatial neglect from homonymous hemianopia in this patient. Does she have one or both, and what is the neuroanatomic explanation?',
      content: '**This patient has BOTH left hemispatial neglect AND a probable left homonymous hemianopia — but the neglect is the dominant and more functionally limiting deficit.**\n\n**Distinguishing the two conditions**:\n\n1. **Homonymous hemianopia (HH)**:\n   - A visual field cut affecting the same side in both eyes (left temporal field of right eye + left nasal field of left eye) due to damage to the right optic radiation or visual cortex.\n   - Patients with pure HH are typically **aware** of the deficit and **compensate** by turning their head/eyes to the affected side.\n   - Confrontation testing shows consistent loss of the left visual field.\n   - Can be confirmed with **formal perimetry** (Humphrey or Goldmann visual field testing).\n\n2. **Hemispatial neglect**:\n   - A failure of **attention** to the contralesional side of space, NOT a primary sensory deficit.\n   - Caused by damage to the **right posterior parietal cortex (inferior parietal lobule)**, **right temporoparietal junction**, or **right inferior frontal gyrus** — areas that form the ventral attention network.\n   - Key distinguishing features in this patient:\n     a. **Extinction on double simultaneous stimulation** — she detects left-sided stimuli when presented alone but misses them when competing right-sided stimuli are present. This is pathognomonic for neglect, not hemianopia.\n     b. **Line bisection deviation to the right** — the patient\'s internal representation of space is shifted rightward. A patient with pure HH would bisect near center or slightly to the left (toward the blind field).\n     c. **Asymmetric cancellation performance** — missing left-sided targets in a visual search task reflects attentional neglect.\n     d. **Clock drawing with right-side crowding** — this is a representational (internal spatial map) deficit, not a visual field cut.\n     e. **Anosognosia** — denial of deficits is characteristic of right hemisphere neglect, not hemianopia.\n\n3. **Neuroimaging correlation**:\n   - A right MCA stroke typically damages the right parietal and temporal cortex. The right **inferior parietal lobule and superior temporal gyrus** are critical for spatial attention. Damage here produces the most severe and persistent neglect.\n   - If the optic radiations (running through the temporal and parietal white matter) are also involved, a left homonymous hemianopia co-occurs.\n   - Order **formal visual field testing (Humphrey perimetry)** to confirm and quantify the hemianopia separately from the neglect.\n\n4. **Why this distinction matters clinically**:\n   - Hemianopia can be partially compensated with consistent head turning — patients with awareness will adapt.\n   - Neglect is much harder to remediate because the patient is **unaware** of what they are missing. They do not spontaneously compensate.\n   - Neglect is the **strongest predictor of poor functional outcome** after stroke, more so than motor weakness.\n   - Driving safety is profoundly impacted by neglect — even if visual fields recover, persistent neglect is an absolute contraindication to driving.',
