    const Section(
      title: '+ What additional standardized assessments would you order to comprehensively evaluate this patient\'s neglect?',
      content: '**Standardized neglect assessments**:\n\n1. **Behavioral Inattention Test (BIT)** — the gold standard comprehensive neglect battery:\n   - **Conventional subtests**: Line crossing, letter cancellation, star cancellation, figure copying, line bisection, representational drawing.\n   - **Behavioral subtests**: Menu reading, article reading, map navigation, address copying, telephone dialing, coin sorting, card sorting.\n   - Provides a composite score with clear cutoffs for clinically significant neglect.\n\n2. **Catherine Bergego Scale (CBS)** — assesses the **functional impact** of neglect in daily activities:\n   - 10 items scored 0-3: grooming (left side of face), dressing (left sleeve/shoe), eating (food on left side of plate), mouth cleaning, gaze orientation, knowledge of left limb, auditory attention to the left, collisions with objects on left, navigation/finding way.\n   - Can be scored by therapist observation (objective) or patient self-report. The **discrepancy** between the two scores quantifies anosognosia.\n\n3. **Formal visual field testing (Humphrey automated perimetry)** — to isolate and quantify the hemianopia separately from attentional neglect.\n\n4. **Functional Independence Measure (FIM)** — to document the impact of neglect on overall functional status and track improvement.\n\n5. **Montreal Cognitive Assessment (MoCA)** — to screen for concurrent executive dysfunction, which commonly co-occurs with neglect after right hemisphere stroke.\n\n6. **Driving-specific assessments** (when appropriate):\n   - **Useful Field of View (UFOV)** test — measures divided attention and processing speed under visual field constraints.\n   - Formal on-road driving evaluation by a certified driver rehabilitation specialist (CDRS) — this is the definitive assessment but should NOT be ordered until neglect has been maximally treated.',
