    const Section(
      title: '+ Describe your comprehensive treatment approach for this patient\'s hemispatial neglect.',
      content: '**A. Visual Scanning Training (Top-Down Approach)**:\n- Systematic training to actively scan leftward during functional tasks.\n- Begin with structured tabletop exercises: reading tasks with a red anchor line on the left margin (\'find the red line before you start reading each line\'), cancellation tasks with progressive difficulty.\n- Progress to functional scanning: teach the patient to scan left before navigating doorways, crossing streets, reaching for items.\n- Use a **lighthouse strategy**: imagine a lighthouse beam sweeping from far left to right before initiating any task.\n- Frequency: daily, 30-45 minute sessions with OT.\n\n**B. Prism Adaptation Therapy (Bottom-Up Approach)**:\n- Patient wears prism glasses that shift the visual field approximately **10 degrees to the right**.\n- During therapy, she performs repeated pointing tasks (reaching for targets) while wearing the prisms. After removal, the **sensorimotor aftereffect** shifts her spatial attention leftward.\n- **Evidence**: Multiple RCTs demonstrate that even brief sessions (20 minutes) of prism adaptation improve neglect symptoms, and effects can persist for hours to weeks.\n- Protocol: 10-20 minutes of prism adaptation daily for 2 weeks, followed by reassessment.\n- Can be combined with scanning training for synergistic effect.\n\n**C. Limb Activation / Spatiomotor Cueing**:\n- Have the patient actively use the **left hand** in left hemispace before initiating tasks. Active movement of the contralesional limb in contralesional space activates the damaged right hemisphere attention networks.\n- Example: Before eating, reach with the left hand to touch the left edge of the tray.\n\n**D. Environmental Modifications**:\n- Position important items (call bell, phone, water) on the LEFT side — this forces the patient to attend leftward. This is counterintuitive but supported by evidence.\n- Place a bright-colored tape strip on the left side of doorframes, wheelchair armrests, and walkways as visual anchors.\n- At meals, turn the plate 180 degrees halfway through the meal, or use a plate with a high-contrast left-side marker.\n\n**E. Technology-Assisted Rehabilitation**:\n- **Virtual reality (VR) neglect training**: Immersive environments that require leftward exploration. Emerging evidence supports improved spatial awareness.\n- **Eye-tracking feedback**: Real-time feedback on gaze patterns can increase awareness of scanning asymmetry.\n\n**F. Addressing Anosognosia**:\n- This is the most critical barrier to rehabilitation. If she does not believe she has a deficit, she will not engage in compensatory strategies.\n- **Video feedback**: Record her performing tasks (eating, walking) and show her the footage. Seeing herself leave food on the left or bump into objects on video can be a powerful tool for building awareness.\n- **Structured confrontation**: During therapy, the therapist guides her to discover the deficit herself (e.g., \'Can you count all the stars on the page? Are you sure you got them all? Let me help you check the left side.\').\n- Avoid argumentative approaches — anosognosia is a neurologic deficit, not a psychological denial. Patience and repeated gentle confrontation over time.\n\n**G. Reassessment timeline**:\n- Repeat BIT and Catherine Bergego Scale at 4 weeks and 3 months.\n- Most neglect recovery occurs in the first 3 months but can continue up to 12 months.\n- Approximately 40% of patients have persistent neglect at 1 year — these patients require long-term compensatory strategies and environmental modifications.',
