      content: '**Driving evaluation and clearance**:\n\n1. **Current driving status**: The patient should NOT be driving at this time. Residual hemispatial neglect, even if improved, poses an unacceptable safety risk for herself and others. I would clearly document in the chart that I have advised her not to drive.\n\n2. **Referral to a Certified Driver Rehabilitation Specialist (CDRS)**:\n   - A CDRS conducts a comprehensive evaluation including: clinical visual/cognitive assessment, behind-the-wheel road test in a dual-controlled vehicle, assessment of reaction time, divided attention, and scanning behavior in real traffic conditions.\n   - The CDRS can also recommend adaptive equipment or vehicle modifications if appropriate.\n   - Locate a CDRS through the Association for Driver Rehabilitation Specialists (ADED) directory.\n\n3. **Pre-driving assessments to complete first**:\n   - **Formal visual field testing** (Humphrey perimetry) — many states require a minimum binocular visual field (typically 110-140 degrees horizontal) for unrestricted licensure.\n   - **Useful Field of View (UFOV)** test — evaluates divided attention and processing speed. An abnormal UFOV is the strongest predictor of on-road driving failure after stroke.\n   - Updated **BIT/Catherine Bergego Scale** — must demonstrate near-normal or normal scores before on-road testing is justified.\n\n4. **Medicolegal considerations**:\n   - **Physician reporting obligations vary by state**. Some states (e.g., California, Pennsylvania) require mandatory physician reporting of conditions that impair driving. Other states have voluntary reporting. Know your state\'s law.\n   - Document the conversation in detail: that you advised against driving, the medical rationale, and that the patient was informed of the risks.\n   - If she drives despite medical advice and causes an accident, the physician may have liability if the counseling was not documented or the appropriate referrals were not made.\n   - Consider involving the family (husband) as an ally in enforcing the driving restriction.\n\n5. **Return to work assessment**:\n   - Teaching requires sustained attention, multitasking, spatial awareness (monitoring an entire classroom), reading/writing, and safety supervision of children. Residual neglect would impair all of these.\n   - Refer for a **Functional Capacity Evaluation (FCE)** tailored to cognitive demands of the job.\n   - Contact her employer\'s HR department (with her consent) to explore **reasonable accommodations** under the ADA — e.g., a classroom aide, reduced class size, preferential seating arrangements.\n   - If she cannot meet the essential functions of the job even with accommodations, discuss **Social Security Disability Insurance (SSDI)** application. She may qualify based on residual cognitive deficits from stroke.\n   - Document work capacity limitations clearly for any disability applications.',
