    const Section(
      title: '+ How do you manage this conversation with a patient who has anosognosia and is refusing to accept driving restrictions?',
      content: 'This is one of the most challenging conversations in rehabilitation medicine because **anosognosia is a neurologic deficit, not stubbornness or denial**. The patient literally cannot perceive her own impairment. I would approach this with patience, empathy, and firm safety boundaries.\n\n**1. Acknowledge her feelings and validate her frustration**:\n\'I hear you, and I understand how frustrating this must be. Driving means independence, and I can see that being told you cannot drive feels like we are taking something important away from you. You have every right to be upset, and I respect that.\'\n\n**2. Use concrete demonstration rather than argument**:\n\'I want to show you something. When I tested your ability to find targets on a page, you found all of them on the right side but missed many on the left side — and you were certain you had found them all. This is what your brain is doing when you drive: there could be a pedestrian, a cyclist, or a car approaching from your left, and your brain would not alert you to it. You would feel completely confident, but the danger would be invisible to you.\'\n\n**3. Involve the family as allies (with the patient present)**:\n\'Mr. [husband\'s name], can you share what you have observed at home?\' (The husband can describe her bumping into things on the left, missing food on the plate — concrete examples from her daily life that she has not been able to recognize on her own.)\n\n**4. Frame this as temporary and offer a path forward**:\n\'I am not saying you will never drive again. What I am saying is that right now, it is not safe — and I would not be doing my job if I let you do something that could hurt you or someone else. Here is my plan: we continue intensive therapy targeting this specific problem. In 4-6 weeks, I will retest you. If your scores improve to the normal range, I will refer you to a driving specialist who will take you out on the road in a car with dual controls and evaluate you in real traffic. If you pass that test, you can drive. I want to help you get there.\'\n\n**5. Address the safety boundary directly and compassionately**:\n\'I need to be honest with you about something important. If you choose to drive against medical advice and something happens, there could be serious legal consequences for you — including loss of your license, liability for any injuries, and potential criminal charges. More importantly, if you hurt someone or yourself, that is something we could have prevented. I care about your safety, and I care about the people on the road.\'\n\n**6. Document and follow through**:\n- Document the conversation in detail in the medical record.\n- Provide written instructions stating she should not drive.\n- If your state has mandatory reporting, follow the appropriate reporting process.\n- Schedule close follow-up (2-4 weeks) to reassess and maintain the therapeutic relationship.\n- Consider a referral to neuropsychology for formal cognitive assessment and additional anosognosia intervention.\n\n**Key principles**: Respect for autonomy balanced with duty to protect (beneficence and non-maleficence), recognition that anosognosia is neurologic not volitional, concrete demonstration over abstract argument, offering a pathway to the desired goal, clear documentation, and knowledge of state-specific reporting obligations.',
